WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.567
As we enter

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:01.067
year two of the

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.502
Russian invasion of Ukraine.

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:04.704
How united is the global community

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:05.972
against Russia?

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.507
Not as much as you might think.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.809
Yes, the West's core coalition remains

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.277
remarkably solid.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.679
But you get a sense of the big picture

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:13.613
when you look at this map

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:14.914
that was published in The Washington

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:16.950
Post, differentiating those countries

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.818
who have armed Ukraine or impose

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:20.220
sanctions on Russia.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.855
And those that have not.

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:23.490
And yes, on Thursday, the U.N.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.591
General Assembly approved

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:25.825
a nonbinding resolution

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:26.626
that calls for Russia

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:28.528
to end hostilities in Ukraine

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.930
and demands the withdrawal of its forces.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.566
But while 141 countries voted in favor,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.236
seven voted against, and 32 abstained,

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.071
as the New York Times

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:38.972
recently summarized,

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:40.640
instead of cleaving in two.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.575
The world has fragmented

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:43.543
a vast middle seas.

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:45.311
Russia's invasion is primarily

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:47.414
a European and American problem.

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:48.415
Rather than view it

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:50.350
as an existential threat.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.751
These countries are largely focused

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.420
on protecting their own interests

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.354
amid the economic

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:55.955
and geopolitical upheaval

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.957
caused by the invasion.

00:00:57.957 --> 00:00:59.225
So against this backdrop,

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.060
have the sanctions

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.662
imposed by 33 countries been effective?

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:05.298
In a piece in Fortune titled

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:06.933
How the Russian Economy

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.535
Self-immolated in the Year

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:10.637
Since Putin Invaded Ukraine.

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:13.706
Jeffrey Sonnenfeld and Steven Tan of Yale

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:15.241
write the 1000

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:16.443
plus global companies

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:17.610
who voluntarily chose

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.712
to exit Russia in an unprecedented

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:21.414
historic mass exodus

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:22.582
in the weeks after February.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:25.151
2022 have largely held true

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:26.386
to their pledges

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:27.854
and have either fully divested

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:29.923
or in the process of fully separating

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.958
from Russia with no plans of return.

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:34.127
Quote, These voluntary business

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:35.261
exits of companies

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:36.796
with in-country revenues

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:39.599
equivalent to 35% of Russia's GDP

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.534
that employ 12% of the country's

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:42.902
workforce were coupled

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:43.770
with the imposition

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.370
of enduring

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.172
international government sanctions

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:47.807
unparalleled in their scale.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:48.942
And scope.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:50.443
So the picture presented

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:51.978
is that it is really crippled

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:53.379
the Russian economy.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.282
But in the same week, Gallup found this

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.719
sanctions failed to sour Russians

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:01.621
outlook on economy.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.656
The poster summarizes more Russians

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.959
see economic conditions improving,

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.427
then getting worse.

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:08.428
Majorities across

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:09.729
all regions are satisfied

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:10.396
with their standard

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:11.397
of living, and regions

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.899
closest to the conflict

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:13.633
have seen huge

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.668
improvements in perceptions.

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:16.936
How can that be?

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:17.470
Joining me now

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.972
to discuss is Muhammad Yunus.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:19.906
He's Gallup's

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:22.308
editor in chief and editor of The Week

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.877
in Charts.

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:25.011
Thanks so much for being here.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:25.678
First of all,

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:28.214
how can you get accurate polling data

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:29.883
from Russia?

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:31.684
Well, we've been polling across

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:33.720
Russia now for 15 years.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.289
It is not as hard as you think,

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:37.423
but it's certainly not easy.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:37.991
And it's part

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:39.392
of what we've been building

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:42.362
since 2005 called the Gallup World Poll.

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:43.563
And it's where we have

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:44.898
now quantified

00:02:44.898 --> 00:02:46.866
the voice of 98%

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.101
of the world's population.

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:50.003
So we've been very hard at work

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:51.204
throughout this conflict

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:53.306
trying to quantify people's voices

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:56.276
both in the Ukraine but also in Russia.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:57.310
In the United States

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.611
and in neighboring countries.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:02.682
What accounts for the relatively rosy

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.651
outlook on their economy

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.686
that you found among the Russian people?

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:08.388
Well,

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:10.290
I don't want to overstate the rosy

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.291
ness of the outlook.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:12.392
Certainly,

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:13.726
Russia and Russians

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:16.062
have paid an enormous cost

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:18.298
both in loss of life and otherwise.

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:20.767
But perceptions of people's own

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.702
economic outlook

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:23.703
aren't as negative

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:26.072
as you would imagine, given

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:28.374
the also likely very accurate

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:29.742
and sound data

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:30.977
that you started this segment

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:32.445
with from economists.

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.013
I think this is why

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.615
giving people a voice in these

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:37.217
situations is so critical,

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:38.384
because it's easy

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.819
for either side to really fall

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:41.321
into the narrative

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.389
of what their view is

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:44.324
from the Western view.

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:45.258
As you mentioned,

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:47.460
Sanctions have been really a cornerstone

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.262
of that policy.

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.030
But if you look at some aspects

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.398
of the Russian economy,

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.434
say, for example, the value of the ruble

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:56.903
Certainly people's own assessments,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.405
we don't see that pain, at least not yet

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.874
settling in on that popular level.

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:04.210
Mohammad, there's another

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.211
matter of perception

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:07.880
that that I'm now having my eyes open to.

00:04:07.981 --> 00:04:09.415
I was under the belief

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.885
that the world is united against Russia,

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:13.553
that it's Russia

00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:14.887
and a couple of rogue states,

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:16.756
you know, the usual suspects, Iran

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:18.491
and North Korea.

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.826
But the data that I pointed out

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:20.460
at the outset

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:21.127
in a great piece

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:21.728
that was published

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:23.029
in both The Washington Post

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:23.463
and another

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:24.197
in The New York Times

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:25.465
opened my eyes to the fact

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:26.499
that particularly

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:27.600
in the Southern Hemisphere,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.569
it's just not the case.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.004
You know, and the other thing

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:32.005
that recently happened

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:32.272
is the

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:33.606
G20 failing

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:36.676
to have a consistent communique around

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.112
the situation in Russia.

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:39.646
You're right,

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:41.147
the world isn't united

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:42.382
because the world is being impacted

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:43.549
in different ways.

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.118
One of the things we do

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:48.421
is track global hunger and also people's

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:50.890
assessments of their own

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.058
economic situations,

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:52.825
not just in Russia,

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:54.360
but across the global South.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.396
And we absolutely see the pain

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:59.565
of inflation, of the conflict,

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:00.366
in particular

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:01.000
in countries

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:01.834
that are

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:03.870
really important grain importers

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.171
from Russia and Ukraine

00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:06.873
seeing the pain of this conflict.

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:08.241
So from the perspective

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.143
of the rest of the world,

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:11.611
it looks at this conflict

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:13.880
in a very different light than the US

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.181
which is really looking

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:16.382
at it from the perspective of

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:18.618
the global order.

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:19.485
And the data

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.254
we just came out of the field with

00:05:21.254 --> 00:05:21.654
I know you're going

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.122
to share really drives home

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:25.525
that point at least here in the US.

00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:26.225
I'm going to be right.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:26.993
We've we've spoken

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:28.328
about the perception of Russians.

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:29.996
We've talked about the global dynamics.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:30.930
Let's put up.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.098
Thank you for giving to us

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:33.066
the very latest

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:35.568
in terms of American perspective

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:36.936
and how folks here

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:38.071
are looking at the war.

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:40.073
What's the bottom line?

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:41.507
The bottom line is we continue

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.343
to see an uptick in Americans

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:46.245
viewing the conflict itself as

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:47.680
an important

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:48.915
or critical threat

00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:49.949
to the vital interests

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:51.584
of the United States.

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:53.419
It started out about in the forties,

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:54.220
in the mid forties,

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.689
when actually the first round of

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:57.590
this recent

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:58.591
flare up between

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:00.159
Ukraine and Russia took place

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:01.294
several years ago.

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.296
But what's important to keep in mind,

00:06:03.296 --> 00:06:05.365
Michael, is both here in the US,

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:06.632
in Russia,

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:09.035
resolve is really there

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:11.070
for a prolonged conflict

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.639
in the Ukraine, its highest of all.

00:06:14.073 --> 00:06:14.941
And we talked about that

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:16.142
last time I was on the program.

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:18.511
So from a public sentiment perspective,

00:06:18.644 --> 00:06:21.114
the critical populations to this conflict

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.615
seem to be preparing

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:24.784
for a much longer haul

00:06:24.784 --> 00:06:26.119
than many of us had hoped when

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:27.053
the conflict started.

